# Your plumbing rules.
#
# This is /usr/inferno/lib/plumbing, kept in ~/.infernode/usr/inferno on
# a desktop install; a user with a home of their own (/usr/<name>) has
# /usr/<name>/lib/plumbing instead.
#
# The plumber reads this file first and then the system's defaults
# (/lib/lucifer/plumbing on the desktop, /lib/xen/plumbing in a
# standalone Xenith).  The first rule that fires wins, so a rule here
# overrides a default, and a message none of yours takes goes on to
# them.  The language is in plumbing(6) (man 6 plumbing);
# /usr/inferno/lib/plumbing.inferno is the set Inferno shipped, to copy
# from.
#
# Edit, then load the rules into the running plumber (the defaults
# named as the boot named them):
#
#	/lib/sh/plumbrules load /lib/lucifer/plumbing
#
# A rule the plumber cannot parse is reported, and the plumber keeps
# the rules it had (at boot it starts on the defaults alone).
#
# For example, web pages in Xenith rather than in Charon:
#
#	kind is text
#	data matches 'https?://[^ ]+'
#	plumb to edit
